FILES
=====
- vector_add.c - the actual workload, includes both scalar and vector addition
                 depending on compiler flags

- Makefile     - the instructions to create the binaries
                 NOTE: I initially noticed that there wasn't much of a difference
                       between scalar and vector, and after some research I found
                       that the compiler automatically vectorizes addition if it
                       can, which makes sense. I added some flags to tell it not
                       to do that.

- vadd_rvv     - the binary compiled for RISC-V Vector Extension (used for benchmarking)

- vadd_scalar  - the binary compiled for standard RISC-V (control case)

NOTE: There are *_check versions of the above two files. These are versions of the
      binaries that I built with functionality checking to make sure that the program
      still worked any time I changed it. This adds overhead instructions which would
      skew the result, so I left it out of the binaries we will actually use for the
      research.

- run_se.py    - the gem5 config script used to set up the architecture that we are
                 testing. I tried to keep as much constant as I could, with the 
                 exception of VLEN and ELEN (not sure if we'll use ELEN). I added
                 some command line parsing to make the sweep easier.

Example gem5 commands
=====================
To run the scalar binary and store the outputs in out_scalar

 ../build/RISCV/gem5.opt -d out_scalar run_se.py vadd_scalar

To run the RVV binary with a VLEN of 256 and store the output in out_rvv256

 ../build/RISCV/gem5.opt -d out_rvv256 run_se.py vadd_rvv --vlen 256




NOTE: To compile binaries with the RISC-V Vector instruction set, you need 
      additional packages, and I don't have sudo on the ECE server, so I 
      configured my local system to do all the building. I left the Makefile
      and c file I used as the workload just in case anyone was curious.

      -Ethan
